    <string name="w4_about_intro">ChromaSol recognizes in a photo the Sanzo Wada color combination closest to the clothing. All analysis runs on device: no network, no accounts, no photo leaves the phone. English color names follow the Ridgway nomenclature as in the dictionary.</string>
    <string name="w4_credit_wada">Sanzo Wada (和田三造, 1883–1967): “A Dictionary of Color Combinations” (配色事典, 1933–1934), 348 combinations from Vol. 1. Public domain in Japan.</string>
    <string name="w4_credit_seigensha">Seigensha Art Publishing: modern edition of the dictionary (2011), reference for the book values.</string>
    <string name="w4_credit_dblodorn">dblodorn: sanzo-wada digital transcription (CMYK values and combinations).</string>
    <string name="w4_credit_mattdesl">mattdesl: dictionary-of-colour-combinations 1.0.2 package (MIT), exact source of data/wada/source/colors.json.</string>
    <string name="w4_credit_room">AndroidX Room and Jetpack Compose (Apache 2.0): SQLite database and UI on Android. On iOS: GRDB.swift (Gwendal Roué, MIT).</string>
    <string name="w4_credit_unity">Unity (ThrowTheSwitch, MIT): C color engine test framework.</string>
    <string name="w4_credit_musl">musl libc (MIT): portable math functions of the engine (cbrt, pow, exp, atan2, sin, cos) so iOS, Android and WASM produce the same bits.</string>
    <string name="w4_credit_ciede">CIEDE2000: Sharma, Wu and Dalal (2005), implementation and reference test set.</string>
    <string name="w4_credit_model">On-device people detector: model bundled in the app, no runtime downloads. On iOS, Vision provides it.</string>
